// A package: every file of a folder is one module, imported by the name of the
// folder, and its files see each other's names with no import. The `public`
// names of all of them are the API of the package: `geo.Sides` comes from
// constants.wsp and `geo.area` from this file, and a name two files both make
// public is an error. A file that should be a module of its own starts with
// `#standalone`, which is how the files next to main.wsp are written. A folder
// inside a package is a package of its own, imported by its own path.
//
// Visibility has three steps. A name with no word belongs to its module, the
// folder. `internal` lends it to every file of this project and hides it from
// the projects that depend on this one. `public` gives it to everyone.

public func area(int width, height) int => width * height;

public func perimeter(int side) int => scale(side);

// A field can carry `private` or `internal` too. This one is private, so only
// this file can read it, write it or name it in a literal. Code elsewhere can
// still hold a struct that was made by a function of this file.
public struct Rect {
  int width;
  int height;
  private int cachedArea;
}

public func newRect(int width, height) Rect {
  return Rect{width: width, height: height, cachedArea: width * height};
}

public func (*Rect r) area() int => r.cachedArea;
